    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks. can u also compare the management styles? like, how do passive vs active management usually play into the cost and the way they handle things like tracking error?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "ETF management styles tend to align with Mutual Funds, but with more flexibility. Passive management, often seen in ETFs, focuses on tracking an index or benchmark at minimum cost, with fees typically lower compared to active management. Actively managed ETFs try to beat the market, but this often comes at a higher cost due to higher fees. Tracking error, or the difference between an ETF's holdings and its benchmark, is more easily managed in passive ETFs, as they aim to extinguish tracking error over time. In contrast, active ETFs strive to outperform their benchmark, which can lead to increased tracking error.",
      "source": "target",
